### What does `distinct()` do?

`distinct()` is an intermediate, stateful operation on a stream. It returns a new stream with duplicate elements removed. Internally it maintains a set of elements it has already emitted, and for each incoming element it checks whether an equal element is already in that set. If yes, the element is dropped; if no, the element passes downstream and is recorded.

The key fact for an interview is that `distinct()` has no built-in notion of what makes two objects duplicates. It asks the element itself through `equals` and `hashCode`.

```java
package com.bank.dedup;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DistinctDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accountTypes = List.of("SAVINGS", "CURRENT", "SAVINGS", "LOAN", "CURRENT");
        List<String> unique = accountTypes.stream()
                .distinct()
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println(unique);
    }
}
```

```text
[SAVINGS, CURRENT, LOAN]
```

### How does `distinct()` rely on `equals` and `hashCode`?

`distinct()` decides equality using the same contract a `HashSet` uses: it computes `hashCode()` to find the bucket and calls `equals()` to confirm. If your class does not override these methods, Java uses identity equality from `Object`, meaning two different instances are never considered equal even if every field matches.

So for a plain class without `equals`/`hashCode`, `distinct()` removes nothing unless you pass the very same reference twice.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// WRONG for distinct-by-value: no equals/hashCode override
public class AccountNoEquals {
    private final long id;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    public AccountNoEquals(long id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }
    // distinct() will keep BOTH of two accounts with id 101
    // because the default equals compares object identity.
}
```

The correct version overrides both methods so value equality works:

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Objects;

public class Account {
    private final long id;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    public Account(long id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public long getId() { return id; }
    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Account)) return false;
        Account other = (Account) o;
        return id == other.id && balance.compareTo(other.balance) == 0;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(id, balance);
    }
}
```

### How do records make `distinct()` work automatically?

A Java record (Java 16+) generates `equals` and `hashCode` from every component. That means `distinct()` treats two records as duplicates only when they are identical in every field. This is the cleanest way to get value-based `distinct()`.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record Employee(long id, String name, String department, BigDecimal salary) { }
```

```java
package com.bank.dedup;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class RecordDistinctDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee a = new Employee(1, "Rajesh", "Trading", new BigDecimal("900000"));
        Employee b = new Employee(1, "Rajesh", "Trading", new BigDecimal("900000"));
        Employee c = new Employee(2, "Priya", "Risk", new BigDecimal("850000"));

        List<Employee> unique = List.of(a, b, c).stream()
                .distinct()
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println(unique.size());
    }
}
```

```text
2
```

Because `a` and `b` are equal in every component, the record's generated `equals` reports them identical and `distinct()` keeps only one.

### How do you remove duplicates by a key such as id?

Real data frequently has duplicates that share a business key while other fields differ. A feed may send the same employee id twice with a stale name on one copy. Here `distinct()` cannot help, because the two objects are not equal. You deduplicate by the key instead.

The stateful-filter technique uses a `Set` of already-seen keys. `Set.add` returns `true` only the first time a key appears, so the filter keeps the first occurrence per key.

```java
package com.bank.dedup;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DedupByKeyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> feed = List.of(
                new Employee(101, "Rajesh Kumar", "Trading", new BigDecimal("900000")),
                new Employee(101, "Rajesh K.", "Trading", new BigDecimal("900000")),
                new Employee(102, "Priya Sharma", "Risk", new BigDecimal("850000"))
        );

        Set<Long> seen = new HashSet<>();
        List<Employee> deduped = feed.stream()
                .filter(e -> seen.add(e.id()))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        deduped.forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.id() + " -> " + e.name()));
    }
}
```

```text
101 -> Rajesh Kumar
102 -> Priya Sharma
```

### How do you deduplicate by a key using `toMap`?

The collector approach builds a `Map` keyed by the business key. A three-argument `toMap` takes a merge function that decides what to do on a key collision. Keeping the first value gives the same result as the stateful filter, and it is safe in parallel.

```java
package com.bank.dedup;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DedupByKeyMapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> feed = List.of(
                new Employee(101, "Rajesh Kumar", "Trading", new BigDecimal("900000")),
                new Employee(101, "Rajesh K.", "Trading", new BigDecimal("900000")),
                new Employee(102, "Priya Sharma", "Risk", new BigDecimal("850000"))
        );

        Map<Long, Employee> byId = feed.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.toMap(Employee::id, e -> e, (first, second) -> first));

        Collection<Employee> deduped = byId.values();
        System.out.println(deduped.size());
    }
}
```

```text
2
```

Without the merge function, `toMap` throws `IllegalStateException` on a duplicate key, so the merge function is required for deduplication.

### How do `distinct()` and key-based dedup compare?

| Aspect | `distinct()` | Stateful filter by key | `toMap` by key |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Removes duplicates by | Full `equals`/`hashCode` | A chosen key | A chosen key |
| Keeps which copy | First by encounter order | First by encounter order | Decided by merge function |
| Needs `equals`/`hashCode` | Yes | No (only on the key) | No (only on the key) |
| Parallel safe | Yes | No | Yes |
| Result type | Stream of elements | Stream of elements | Map, then `values()` |

```java
// Side by side: same feed, two strategies
List<Employee> exact = feed.stream().distinct().collect(Collectors.toList());

Set<Long> seen = new HashSet<>();
List<Employee> byKey = feed.stream()
        .filter(e -> seen.add(e.id()))
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is expecting `distinct()` to remove key-duplicates. It will not, because the objects are not equal.

```java
// WRONG: two records with the same id but different name are NOT equal,
// so distinct keeps both.
List<Employee> result = feed.stream().distinct().collect(Collectors.toList());
// result still contains both id=101 rows
```

```java
// CORRECT: dedupe on the id key explicitly.
Set<Long> seen = new HashSet<>();
List<Employee> result = feed.stream()
        .filter(e -> seen.add(e.id()))
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

The second mistake is reusing a stateful filter in a parallel stream, which produces non-deterministic output because threads race on the shared `Set`.

```java
// WRONG: shared mutable Set in a parallel stream.
Set<Long> seen = new HashSet<>();
List<Employee> result = feed.parallelStream()
        .filter(e -> seen.add(e.id())) // data race, unpredictable result
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

```java
// CORRECT: use toMap, which is parallel safe.
List<Employee> result = new ArrayList<>(feed.parallelStream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(Employee::id, e -> e, (a, b) -> a))
        .values());
```

### What are the edge cases and senior nuances?

On an ordered stream, `distinct()` preserves the encounter order of the first occurrence. The stateful filter also keeps the first occurrence in a sequential stream. The `toMap` approach loses encounter order because a `HashMap` has no order; to preserve it, supply `LinkedHashMap::new` as the map factory.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;

// Preserve first-seen order while deduping by key
Map<Long, Employee> byId = feed.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                Employee::id,
                e -> e,
                (first, second) -> first,
                LinkedHashMap::new));
```

Another edge case is that `BigDecimal` equality by `equals` is scale-sensitive: `new BigDecimal("1.0").equals(new BigDecimal("1.00"))` is `false`. If money fields drive your `equals`, use `compareTo` in the body so `distinct()` treats equal values as equal regardless of scale.

### When should you use each approach?

Use `distinct()` when a duplicate means identical in every field, which is the common case for records and well-defined value objects. Use the stateful filter in a sequential stream when you want the first row per business key and prefer a one-liner. Use `toMap` when you run in parallel, when you want control over which duplicate to keep through the merge function, or when you already need the result as a map keyed by id.

```java
// Keep the HIGHER salary on an id collision instead of the first
Map<Long, Employee> byId = feed.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                Employee::id,
                e -> e,
                (a, b) -> a.salary().compareTo(b.salary()) >= 0 ? a : b));
```
